use std
into fold_check.amni

```amni
import os;
import gfx;
import math;
import gfx.linalg;
import gfx.gltf;
import gfx.motion;
import gfx.scene3d;
import clips;
import mix;
```

Vertex helpers. Each vertex g starts at `b = g * gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS` in `gltf::verts`. `vp(g int) float3` is the rest point (gltf::verts[b], gltf::verts[b + 1], gltf::verts[b + 2]) and `vn(g int) float3` the rest normal at b + 3, b + 4, b + 5. `skp(g int, m gfx::mat4[:]) float3` is the linear-blend skinned point: start from zero and for q in 4, with `w = gltf::verts[b + 22 + q]`, when w > 0 add `linalg::point(m[gltf::verts[b + 18 + q] as int], vp(g)) * w`. `skn(g int, m gfx::mat4[:]) float3` is the skinned normal: with p = vp(g) and n = vn(g), for the same four influences add `(linalg::point(mm, p + n) - linalg::point(mm, p)) * w` where mm is that joint's matrix, then return `linalg::unit3` of the sum.

Globals: `bt int[>..]` (three global vertex ids per skin triangle), `ra f32[>..]` (rest area per triangle), `rb int[>..]` (body region per triangle), `let NB = 8` regions, `var h0 f32 = 1.0e30` and `var h1 f32 = -1.0e30` (rest height range of the skin vertices), `pal gfx::mat4[>..]` (joint matrices), `var sk_i = -1` (the skin index) and `var dump = false`.

`region(name const byte[:]) int` maps a joint name to a body region by its prefix: 0 for "pelvis" or "thigh", 1 for "calf", "foot" or "ball", 2 for "spine", 3 for "clavicle" or "upperarm", 4 for "lowerarm", 5 for "hand", "index", "middle", "pinky", "ring" or "thumb", 6 for "neck" or "head", else 7. `rname(r int) const byte[:]` returns "hips+thighs", "legs", "torso", "shoulders", "forearms", "hands", "neck+head" or "other" for 0 to 7.

`gather(asset int, name const byte[:])` collects one mesh. `let nd = gltf::find_node(asset, name)`; when nd < 0 print "no node " and the name and return. Set `sk_i = gltf::nodes[nd].skin`. With `let ms = gltf::meshes[gltf::nodes[nd].mesh]`, for pi in ms.prims.count take `let pr = gltf::prims[ms.prims.first + pi]`: for v in pr.vcount update h0 and h1 with the min and max of `vp(pr.vfirst + v).y`; for q in pr.icount push `pr.vfirst + gltf::indices[pr.ifirst + q] as int` onto bt. Then for each triangle t (corners bt[3t], bt[3t + 1], bt[3t + 2] with rest points a, b, c) push the rest area `0.5 * linalg::length3(linalg::cross(b - a, c - a))` onto ra and its region onto rb: sum the weights per joint slot over the three corners (corner g has joint slots `gltf::verts[g * gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS + gltf::AT_JOINT + k] as int` with weights `gltf::verts[g * gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS + gltf::AT_WEIGHT + k]` for k in 4), take the slot with the largest sum, its node `gltf::ints[gltf::skins[sk_i].joints.first + slot]`, and push `region(gltf::text_of(gltf::nodes[node].name))`.

`measure(it int, tag const byte[:])` checks the current pose. Call `s3d::pose(it)` then `motion::joints(sk_i, pal)`. Make three local `int[>..]` arrays fo, cr and st, each resized to NB zeros, and totals F, C, X. For each triangle t with ra[t] > 1.0e-10: skinned corner points pa, pb, pc from `skp(id, pal[..])`; `ng = linalg::cross(pb - pa, pc - pa)`; area `0.5 * linalg::length3(ng)`; reference normal `nr = skn(i0, pal[..]) + skn(i1, pal[..]) + skn(i2, pal[..])`. The triangle is folded when `linalg::dot3(ng, nr) < 0.0`, crushed when area < 0.3 * ra[t], stretched when area > 2.5 * ra[t]; count each in its region rb[t] and in its total; when dump is true, the tag starts with "Sitting_Idle_Loop 2" and rb[t] == 0, print "F " (folded) or "C " (crushed, when not folded) for such triangles followed by the rest centroid x, y and z separated by spaces. Print `tag, " folded ", F, " crushed ", C, " stretched ", X, " of ", bt.len / 3`. Then for each region k from 0 to NB - 1 where fo[k] + cr[k] + st[k] > 0, print `"  ", rname(k), " folded ", fo[k], " crushed ", cr[k], " stretched ", st[k]`.

`world_rot(nd int) linalg::Quat` is the bind-pose world rotation of node nd: the product of `gltf::nodes[k].r` from the root down to nd, walking `gltf::nodes[k].parent` up (negative ends) and composing with `linalg::qmul(parent, child)`.

`move_joint(asset int, spec const byte[:])` moves one joint's pivot in the bind pose while every other joint keeps its world position. spec is "name:dx,dy,dz" in meters, world axes; split it at the colon and the commas and parse the reals with `parse_flt`. Find the node with `gltf::find_node(asset, name)` (print "no joint " and the name and return when negative). Let d be the world delta. Add `linalg::qrotate(linalg::qconj(world_rot(parent)), d)` to `gltf::nodes[nd].t` (use the identity rotation when the parent is negative). For each child c (`gltf::ints[ch.first + k]` for k in `gltf::nodes[nd].children.count`) subtract `linalg::qrotate(linalg::qconj(world_rot(nd)), d)` from `gltf::nodes[c].t`. For every skin s of the asset (`gltf::assets[asset].skins`, global index first plus k) and every joint slot k of it whose node `gltf::ints[gltf::skins[s].joints.first + k]` is nd, replace the inverse bind m = `gltf::matrices[gltf::skins[s].inverse_binds.first + k]` with `gfx::mul(gfx::translate(float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 } - (linalg::point(m, d) - linalg::point(m, float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 }))), m)`. Print "moved " and the name.

`find_clip(name const byte[:], n int) int` returns the first clip c below n whose name equals name, else -1: for each c clear a reusable `byte[>..]` buffer, call `clips::clips_name(c, buf)` and compare `buf == name`.

`main()`. `let a = args()`; when `a.len < 3` print "usage: fold_check <model.glb> <clips.gltf> [mesh=skin] [move=joint:dx,dy,dz ...] [dump]; counts folded, crushed and stretched triangles per body region in bind, idle, walk, dance and sit poses" and return; `let asset = gltf::load(a[1])` (when negative print "load fail" and return); for every argument from index 3 on that starts with "move=", `move_joint(asset, the rest of it)`; then `let it = s3d::add(asset)`; `let nlib = clips::clips_load(a[2], asset)`. Build the slot table exactly so: `var sl i32[22]; let first = gltf::assets[asset].nodes.first; for i in 22 { sl[i] = if clips::kc_slot[i] < 0 { -1 } else { (clips::kc_slot[i] as int - first) as i32 }; }`, then call the unqualified `mix_open(asset, it, sl[..])` and for c in nlib the unqualified `mix_clip(clips::kc_nf[c], clips::kc_loop[c], clips::clips_frames(c))`. Set dump when any argument from index 3 on equals "dump". The mesh name is the first argument from index 3 on that neither starts with "move=" nor equals "dump", else "skin". Call `gather(asset, that name)`; when bt is empty return. Print `"skin tris ", bt.len / 3, " height ", h0, " to ", h1, " clips ", nlib`. Call `measure(it, "bind")`. Then the clip schedule: names `["Idle_Loop", "Walk_Formal_Loop", "Dance_Loop", "Sitting_Idle_Loop"]` with sample times `[[1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0], [0.5, 1.0, 0.0, 0.0], [0.7, 1.4, 2.1, 2.8], [1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 0.0]]`. For each clip index ci: `let c = find_clip(names[ci], nlib)`; when c < 0 print "no clip " and the name and continue; call the unqualified `mix_play(c, 0.0)` and keep a local time `var t f32 = 0.0`; for si in 4 with `let target = at[ci][si]` (skip when target <= 0.0): while `t + 0.0001 < target` call the unqualified `mix_advance(1.0 / 60.0)` and add 1.0 / 60.0 to t; then build a tag (the clip name, a space, `str(target)`) and call `measure(it, tag)`.
